#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229183 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c652164a-2e94-5e16-a410-5143037af134" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Amount at an Overflow Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "635d2341-3a33-5ac9-bc90-9aa868281876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385210} {:answer/id #uuid "923fae5f-f36e-59d7-a2f9-4b1a32154401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385211} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe85108-505b-58b7-a19a-be78c15e1fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385212} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc28125-aa6b-5b7d-9978-9da8bc0bd171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385213} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd0d588-4274-52c3-9c9a-c8c45c106ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fe85108-505b-58b7-a19a-be78c15e1fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385212} :answer-field/id #uuid "711b7022-c824-5eae-90a8-e7d790b19008" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c6933cf-ef37-5bf5-8816-b51b8e56e9df" :question/math-academy-id "q-336501" :question/problem "A dye mixing tank at a textile factory can hold up to $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $100\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution with $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved dye. A more concentrated dye solution with concentration $0.6\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.6\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =9\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $100\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $15\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=100+15t-10t=5(20+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(20+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{5(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =9-\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q=9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(20+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(20+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(20+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q) & =(20+t)^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\ (20+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(20+t)Q & =9(20+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2}) & =9(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 9(20+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(20+t)^{2} & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(20+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3(20+t)+\\frac{C}{(20+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=20,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =20 \\\\ 3(20+0)+\\frac{C}{(20+0)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ 60+\\frac{C}{(20)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(20)^{2}} & =-40 \\\\ C & =-40(20)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3(20+t)-\\frac{40(20)^{2}}{(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3(20+t)-40{(\\frac{20}{20+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $180\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=180$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =180 \\\\ 100+15{t}_{o}-10{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 100+5{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =80 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =16\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(16) & =3(20+16)-40{(\\frac{20}{20+16})}^{2} \\\\  & =108-40{(\\frac{20}{36})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈95.7\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229182 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ab1d837-f782-5439-8e25-3c764f9cb5c5" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Dilution Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a3f26d4-7fd0-5dfc-b30a-66eaf31e8b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.34\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377251} {:answer/id #uuid "59e1559d-1a25-5f5b-86a0-b44ad480a144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.94\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377252} {:answer/id #uuid "6884f190-c170-5c58-85a7-6a4669b950fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.04\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377253} {:answer/id #uuid "3dca446e-f8bb-5a17-9561-bc2230c8306c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.42\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377254} {:answer/id #uuid "9010ad44-b315-5215-8406-035745dd6ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.01\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6884f190-c170-5c58-85a7-6a4669b950fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.04\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377253} :answer-field/id #uuid "753683c1-dfc0-5785-8a4b-d335ca58b088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "01365019-dd55-56aa-aa06-e538a69722fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-336743" :question/problem "A processing tank initially holds $420\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved preservative.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the preservative is pumped into the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of preservative in the tank $15$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of preservative in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which preservative enters the tank minus the rate at which preservative leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which preservative enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the preservative enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *preservative* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which preservative exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which preservative leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $420\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $18\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n420+18t-12t=(420+6t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of preservative in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{420+6t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *preservative* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{420+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.9-\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q=0.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(70+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|70+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(70+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(70+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(70+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q) & =(70+t)^{2}\\cdot 0.9 \\\\ (70+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(70+t)Q & =0.9(70+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2}) & =0.9(70+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 0.9(70+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(70+t)^{2} & =0.9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(70+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.3(70+t)+\\frac{C}{(70+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of preservative. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ 0.3(70+0)+\\frac{C}{(70+0)^{2}} & =10 \\\\ 21+\\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =-11 \\\\ C & =-11(70)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.3(70+t)-\\frac{11(70)^{2}}{(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.3(70+t)-11{(\\frac{70}{70+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of preservative in the tank $15$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(15) & =0.3(70+15)-11{(\\frac{70}{70+15})}^{2} \\\\  & =25.5-11{(\\frac{70}{85})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈18.04\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}]}
